Legal Operations Playbook

A working guide for how the Legal Operations function runs the business of law: the processes, standards, roles and routines that let the legal department deliver faster, spend smarter and manage risk consistently. Adapt the bracketed items [like this] to your organization.
Owner: [Head of Legal Operations] Executive sponsor: [General Counsel] Version: 1.0 Review cadence: Annually, or after any major change in structure, systems or strategy

1. Purpose and Scope

Purpose. Give the legal department a single, practical reference for how work is received, assigned, managed, measured and improved.
This playbook covers:
• Operating model, roles and governance
• Intake, triage and matter management
• Outside counsel and vendor management
• Contract lifecycle management
• Budgeting, spend control and billing
• Technology and data
• Knowledge management and templates
• Reporting, KPIs and continuous improvement
• Change management and communication
This playbook does not replace legal advice, professional-responsibility obligations, or company policies. Where this document conflicts with law or company policy, those take precedence.

2. Mission and Guiding Principles

Mission. Enable Legal to deliver high-quality, cost-effective legal services that protect and advance the business.

Principles
1.  Lawyers practice law. Move routine, administrative and repeatable work away from attorneys through process, templates, automation or delegation.
2.  Standardize first, then automate. Fix a broken process before putting technology on top of it.
3.  Make the easy path the right path. A single front door and clear self-service options beat policing.
4.  Decide with data. Track a small set of reliable metrics and act on them.
5.  Partner with the business. Measure success by internal-client outcomes, not legal activity.
6.  Protect privilege and confidentiality in every tool, workflow and report.
7.  Improve continuously. Review, learn and adjust in regular cycles.

