9.2 Technology Selection Framework

Evaluate any new tool against these questions:
1.  Which specific problem or KPI will this improve, and how will we measure it?
2.  Can we fix the process first without technology?
3.  Does it integrate with our existing systems?
4.  What are the security, privacy and data-residency requirements, and does it pass IT security review?
5.  What is the total cost (licenses, implementation, change management, support)?
6.  Who will own it, and what will adoption look like for users?
7.  What is the exit plan, including data portability?

9.3 AI and Automation Governance

• Use only approved tools for any work involving confidential, privileged or personal data.
• Do not enter privileged or sensitive data into tools that have not passed security and privacy review.
• A qualified attorney reviews AI-generated legal work before it is relied upon or shared.
• Document use cases, risks, data handling and review requirements for each approved AI tool.
• Track accuracy issues, escalate incidents and revisit approvals [at least annually].
• Follow applicable law, professional-responsibility rules and company policy on AI use.

